            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ index of x in old array is <= -1 
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1);

            // another assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ x contains in old array is true 
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX == true);

		// a simple assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(.*?\);" that  @@@ array's count is increasing
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);

		// assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(.*?\);" that  @@@ index of x in new array is the same as old one
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX);
	
		// another assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(.*?\);" that  @@@ array's count is increasing
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);

		// assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(.*?\);" that array contains the new element added
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX == true);   
 
        }
    }
}

For this particular case I think the contract could be violated if we try to add elements that already exist in the array. The specification of List.Add method is that it ensures to add a unique value, so it will return a value such as index of new element if it's inserted, or -1 if the given value is already in the array. 
If the return value of this method is supposed to be index of the element, then it should be between 0 and Count - 1, not -1. And as for the fact that "@@@ index of x in old array is >= -1" - it would be better to check that it is between 0 and Count-1 to avoid violation of pre-conditions also.

So, overall the contract checking in the contract assertion is not correct on the last 2 assertion line due to its inconsistency with the specification of Add method.

Note: I think the "Test" and "ContractTest" word is used interchangeably. In NUnit terminology, we often call unit tests that verify that the preconditions/postconditions of the method have been met "Contract-tests", or simply "Contracts". However, this use is somewhat general and may vary with the specifics of their usage.


A: You're correct. The contract is broken. The Add method does not return -1 when adding a value that is already in the list, but rather, it returns the index in the list where it was found. So, if the input value is in the list, the ContractAssertion will fail. 
I recommend making the assertion more general, or creating a separate ContractTest where you only pass in elements that are already in the list, to test the behaviour when the element is already in the list. 
In practice, you would only use these specific Pex testing utilities for complex logic that isn't immediately clear from the method signatures, and then you would write your own test cases to verify that the logic is correct. However, there are times when these utilities can be very handy, so it's good to be aware of what they're doing and when to use them.

Regarding your comment about the use of "Test" and "ContractTest" interchangably: in NUnit terminology, there are indeed distinct differences between them - test cases are used for verifying a particular behavior, while contract tests are for verifying that your code abides by a set of pre-written rules (usually contractual obligations). 
Your code is currently more along the lines of contract testing than unit testing, because it's more focused on testing specific properties of the Add method rather than verifying that the method behaves as specified in general, given a certain kind of input.

So back to the original question, why do your contract assertions fail?
Adding elements to a list in .NET via the list `Add` method does not return -1 when the element is already in the list. Instead, it returns the index of that element, which is a contract violation for your list. So you might have to create a new testing fixture specifically for tests involving elements already in your list.
Also, if a precondition was violated your process has already begun, so you have no chance to observe the--------------------  	    Assert.LessOrEqual(PexObserve.ValueForAssertion("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x"), -1); 
	    // addition to ensure the object invariants: the final array list count should be more than the original
            Assert.Greater(PexObserve.ValueForAssertion("$new_arrList_Count"), PexObserve.ValueForAssertion("$old_arrList_Count"));
	    // ensure x has been added at the correct index on the new array  @@@ is equal to  @@@
	    Assert.AreEqual(PexObserve.ValueForAssertion("$new_ret"), PexObserve.ValueForAssertion("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x"));
          
            // an assertion that ensures the object invariant: x should now exist in the new arrList
            Assert.IsTrue(PexObserve.ValueForAssertion("$new_arrList_contains_x"));
        }
    }
